# ========================
# OS
# ========================

.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# live scores are regenerated every poll; never worth committing
data/live_scores.json
.last_live_commit
.last_daily_publish
.live_trouble_since
.fantasy_live_state.json

# Fantasy player DB build artifacts (regenerable via `python -m fantasy.data_manager`)
data/fantasy/players/
# Weekly league-scored points: pulled straight back out of nflverse by
# `python -m fantasy.projections --refresh`, so cache, not source.
data/fantasy/points/
# The projection board (data/fantasy/projections/<year>.parquet) is NOT
# ignored, deliberately: it is built once and then read by every power
# build, and a copy built per machine drifts - the Pi built one on 2026-08-21
# with no ADP board on disk, anchored nothing on the market, and ran the
# power rankings on it for three weeks. One committed board, refreshed on
# purpose with `python -m fantasy.projections --refresh`, is what every
# machine simulates on.

# Refetched at the start of every WNBA run (wnba_remaining.wnba_update calls
# wnba_fantasy.fetch_and_save before reading it), and the live job runs every
# 10 minutes — 3.5MB rewritten ~11x a day had accumulated 448MB of history,
# the single largest thing in this repo.
data/wnba/wnba_fantasy_data.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Generated site output. The Pi rebuilds all of this on every run and uploads
# it with `wrangler pages deploy` — git is not in the publish path, so these
# were committed for one reason only: so the PC could see what the Pi
# built. That cost about 100 MB of history a month (one 4 MB page rewritten
# 422 times in 30 days), so the PC uses `pi pull-site` instead.
#
# What stays tracked: the code, the templates, the real assets, the per-day
# prediction archives, and the ~2 MB of archives that ARE the record of what
# the page said. Those can never be regenerated; none of this can't.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
docs/index.html
docs/**/index.html
# Three section homes nothing in the daily run rebuilds - they would be
# deleted on the next pull and never come back.
!docs/men/index.html
!docs/women/index.html
!docs/wnba/index.html
docs/assets/images/charts/
docs/assets/images/share/
docs/assets/favourite-teams.json
docs/fantasy/players-index.json
docs/fantasy/week-projections.json
docs/fantasy/espn-ids.json
docs/fantasy/stakes.json
docs/cfb/stakes.json
docs/fantasy/playoff-picture.json
docs/cfb/playoff-picture.json
docs/fantasy/season-board.json
docs/_includes/cfb_bets.html
docs/_includes/nfl_bets.html
docs/_includes/cfb_top25.html
docs/_includes/wnba_dashboard.html
docs/_includes/wnba_scoreboard.html
docs/_data/cfb_countdown.json
# NOT countdowns.yml: nothing writes it. It is hand-maintained source that
# cbb.render.render_home reads back, and ignoring it broke the homepage
# render on the first deploy after this change. "A generator mentions the
# filename" is not the test - "a generator writes it" is.
docs/cfb/schedule/week-*.html
# Every week of each league's matchups but the current one, fetched by the
# page when its tab is tapped (gordstats.matchup_page.write_weeks).
docs/*/matchups/week-*.html
docs/fantasy/week-context.json
docs/fantasy/strength.json
# Each sport guide's games, merged by the all-sports guide (gordstats.watch_all).
docs/cfb/watch/games.json
docs/nfl/watch/games.json
docs/cbb/watch/games.json
docs/cfb/week-games.json
docs/cbb/star-teams.json
docs/fantasy/recap/latest.json
docs/cfb/recap/latest.json
# Written every live tick by cbb.lines; the daily run copies it to data/cbb/lines/.
data/cbb/lines_live/
# The Pi's untracked copy of each pre-kickoff projection (gordstats.pregame):
# it survives the live tick's `git checkout -- docs data`, which a number kept
# between hourly commits did not.
data/*/gs_proj_live/
# College basketball's pages, rewritten by every in-season daily run
# (gordstats.daily._cbb, CBB_TIPOFF Nov 1 to season end): the two conference
# pages (~320 KB each, cbb.render.render_conferences) and the day's
# bracketology page (predict_<date>.html, cbb.predictions). pandas Styler
# stamps random T_xxxxx ids, so every run would commit a whole-file rewrite.
# The dated pages' content is kept as JSON in docs/assets/data/predictions/
# (cbb.tools.archive_predictions, which deletes all but the newest page) -
# that stays tracked. NOT history.html: six hand-written lines, nothing
# writes it.
#
# Ignoring does not untrack. predict_2026-03-15.html leaves git by itself on
# the first in-season run (the archive deletes it, the Pi commits that). The
# conference pages need `git rm --cached` - but only once an in-season run has
# rewritten them: the commit deletes them from the Pi's checkout on its next
# pull, and before Nov 1 nothing would bring them back.
docs/men/conference.html
docs/women/conference.html
docs/men/predict_*.html
docs/women/predict_*.html
# Caches that are not archives: cfb.boxscores (ESPN box scores, keyless) and
# cfb.usage (CFBD, 3 calls a played week). Each run fetches whatever the file
# lacks, so a fresh clone - or a Pi that lost them - rebuilds the season in
# one run. Tracked, each was rewritten whole after every game, and git cannot
# delta a parquet file. Ignored files survive the deploy's `git checkout -- data`.
data/cfb/cache/
# A data file being written (cfb.partitions writes <name>.partial, then
# renames it into place).
*.partial
